WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.245 --> 00:00:05.356
Hey there! Welcome to "Mathematical Experiments".

00:00:05.356 --> 00:00:08.979
Today, I want to tell you about finite fields.

00:00:08.979 --> 00:00:14.472
It's one of the coolest and most intriguing topics in mathematics.

00:00:14.472 --> 00:00:17.261
And I have quite an ambitious goal:

00:00:17.261 --> 00:00:21.907
to prove the existence of fields of size p^k,

00:00:21.907 --> 00:00:33.591
and also show how we can use Machine Learning to discover and guess the quadratic reciprocity law.

00:00:33.591 --> 00:00:37.222
Of course, there are many interesting videos on this topic on YouTube.

00:00:37.222 --> 00:00:40.579
And here's my list of recommendations.

00:00:40.579 --> 00:00:45.892
The Mathologer channel. Specifically, this video about finite fields and the quadratic reciprocity law.

00:00:45.892 --> 00:00:48.736
It's in English, but you might find a translation.

00:00:48.736 --> 00:00:53.407
You can also use the Yandex Browser translator.

00:00:53.407 --> 00:00:55.417
Alexey Savvateev

00:00:55.417 --> 00:00:58.336
Also, two lecture series.

00:00:58.336 --> 00:01:00.515
I recommend watching it.

00:01:00.515 --> 00:01:10.645
He's always very enthusiastic, with a lot of inspiration, talking about numbers, Cardano's formulas and more.

00:01:10.645 --> 00:01:13.389
And one last recommendation.

00:01:13.389 --> 00:01:15.999
With only 11 thousand views...

00:01:15.999 --> 00:01:19.748
It should have more, because it's a really great algebra course.

00:01:19.748 --> 00:01:24.528
It's not only about number fields, but also about simpler algebraic structures,

00:01:24.528 --> 00:01:26.863
which are usually the starting point for algebra courses.

00:01:26.863 --> 00:01:31.880
Semigroups, groups, modules and number fields too.

00:01:31.880 --> 00:01:36.968
So... I'm going to tell you in my way.

00:01:36.968 --> 00:01:39.578
What are "numbers"?

00:01:39.578 --> 00:01:42.977
Let me list some facts that you might already know.

00:01:42.977 --> 00:01:45.473
We have rational numbers,

00:01:45.473 --> 00:01:47.145
real numbers,

00:01:47.145 --> 00:01:49.186
complex numbers.

00:01:49.186 --> 00:01:54.966
Objects in these three sets can be multiplied, added, divided, and subtracted.

00:01:54.966 --> 00:02:01.816
Here's the next example. This is the ratio of two polynomials, where Q is not equal to zero.

00:02:01.816 --> 00:02:07.075
And, of course, we need to fix the field of coefficients for this polynomial.

00:02:07.075 --> 00:02:11.594
Here's how these three fields are denoted.

00:02:11.594 --> 00:02:17.379
We can write such relationships between these three fields.

00:02:17.379 --> 00:02:19.159
Alright.

00:02:19.159 --> 00:02:24.917
Apart from fields, where we can add, multiply, subtract and divide,

00:02:24.917 --> 00:02:28.330
there are sets like natural numbers.

00:02:28.330 --> 00:02:30.167
There's a serious issue with subtraction there.

00:02:30.167 --> 00:02:35.456
When you do 2 - 10, you end up outside the realm of natural numbers.

00:02:35.456 --> 00:02:41.850
Z doesn't have a problem with subtraction, but it has an issue with division.

00:02:41.850 --> 00:02:47.709
You can further define polynomials with coefficients from Z.

00:02:47.709 --> 00:02:54.167
These can also be multiplied and added, creating objects within the same set, but you can't divide.

00:02:54.167 --> 00:02:58.504
Interesting fact from the future, so to speak:

00:02:58.504 --> 00:03:05.461
сomplex numbers can be constructed as a certain equivalence class

00:03:05.461 --> 00:03:09.143
in the ring of polynomials over real numbers.

00:03:09.143 --> 00:03:13.707
But that's probably a bit complex words. Let's skip them.

00:03:13.707 --> 00:03:21.977
Today, I want to tell you about something where you can perform addition and multiplication on a finite set of objects,

00:03:21.977 --> 00:03:27.192
creating a structure we're familiar with. The structure of numbers.

00:03:27.192 --> 00:03:33.620
These finite number fields are denoted as F2, F3, F4, F5, and so on.

00:03:33.620 --> 00:03:35.736
But there's no F6, for example.

00:03:35.736 --> 00:03:37.710
And no F10.

00:03:37.710 --> 00:03:44.200
There are fields of size p (where p is prime) and size p^k.

00:03:44.200 --> 00:03:48.908
And these finite fields can't be embedded in this sequence,

00:03:48.908 --> 00:03:59.523
but they can be constructed either from Z or from polynomials with coefficients from Z.

00:03:59.523 --> 00:04:01.998
The details are complex.

00:04:01.998 --> 00:04:05.713
But it's a topic accessible to school students.

00:04:05.713 --> 00:04:08.759
And you don't need to know all these fancy words.

00:04:08.759 --> 00:04:15.489
To define multiplication and addition, you need to define addition and multiplication tables.

00:04:15.489 --> 00:04:22.553
Here, I have an example where I took numbers from 0 to 12 and defined addition for them.

00:04:22.553 --> 00:04:28.479
For example, 3 + 1. Look at the intersection, we see 4. That's the answer.

00:04:28.479 --> 00:04:30.319
3 + 1 = 4

00:04:30.319 --> 00:04:31.589
Surprise!

00:04:31.589 --> 00:04:32.726
But here's something more interesting.

00:04:32.726 --> 00:04:37.559
For example, 7 + 6 = 0

00:04:37.559 --> 00:04:46.131
We don't need to go beyond these 13 objects, so the number 13 is simply not here.

00:04:46.131 --> 00:04:50.702
Or rather, it's not really a number; it's just a symbol for an object.

00:04:50.702 --> 00:04:53.587
12 -&gt; 11

00:04:53.587 --> 00:04:55.617
This table is really simple.

00:04:55.617 --> 00:05:01.677
We add the two initial numbers, treating them not just as symbols, but as numbers,

00:05:01.677 --> 00:05:04.916
but we record the result modulo 13.

00:05:04.916 --> 00:05:09.301
"By modulo" means - we take the remainder after dividing by 13.

00:05:09.301 --> 00:05:12.000
You probably already know all this.

00:05:12.000 --> 00:05:16.184
Here's the multiplication table, built using the same principle.

00:05:16.184 --> 00:05:20.762
This creates such edges, consisting of zeros, because 0 times any number is zero.

00:05:20.762 --> 00:05:25.348
And in the middle, we get this interesting table.

00:05:25.348 --> 00:05:29.331
The multiplication table of non-zero numbers modulo 13.

00:05:29.331 --> 00:05:36.000
I'm saying that this is quite a good, successful definition of addition and multiplication,

00:05:36.000 --> 00:05:42.167
because they satisfy laws.

00:05:42.167 --> 00:05:46.284
This law is called the "Associativity Law of Addition".

00:05:46.284 --> 00:05:49.371
That, when you add 3 objects, you can rearrange the parentheses.

00:05:49.371 --> 00:05:52.885
The commutative law. a + b = b + a

00:05:52.885 --> 00:05:55.462
The fact that we have a zero.

00:05:55.462 --> 00:06:02.761
And that for any number, you can find a number "b", where the sum with it is zero.

00:06:02.761 --> 00:06:06.000
And very similar laws for multiplication,

00:06:06.000 --> 00:06:08.878
which I denote with an asterisk.

00:06:08.878 --> 00:06:16.579
And there's also an interesting law that applies to both these laws. Both addition and multiplication.

00:06:16.579 --> 00:06:24.372
And for multiplication, it's written here that just like for addition, there's an inverse.

00:06:24.372 --> 00:06:28.757
But here we get 1, not 0.

00:06:28.757 --> 00:06:34.817
And also a ≠ 0. Inverse elements exist only for numbers not equal to zero.

00:06:34.817 --> 00:06:41.848
These are the laws. And when your addition and multiplication

00:06:41.848 --> 00:06:48.000
have these properties, you can say that you've got a number field.

00:06:48.000 --> 00:06:52.289
All of this might seem a bit dry...

00:06:52.289 --> 00:06:57.579
but you need to overcome this only once, and then it will get interesting.

00:06:57.579 --> 00:07:02.098
We've defined addition and multiplication, but we haven't defined subtraction.

00:07:02.098 --> 00:07:03.803
How do we do that?

00:07:03.803 --> 00:07:04.973
Very simply.

00:07:04.973 --> 00:07:10.062
Because here it's written that for any number "a", there's a "b" such that a + b = 0,

00:07:10.062 --> 00:07:13.849
let's say that b = -a.

00:07:13.849 --> 00:07:23.794
And subtraction "a - c" actually means that we're adding the additive inverse of c to a.

00:07:23.794 --> 00:07:24.930
And division?

00:07:24.930 --> 00:07:26.405
You've probably already guessed.

00:07:26.405 --> 00:07:31.025
a/c = a * c^-1

00:07:31.025 --> 00:07:36.348
-1 power means we're taking the multiplicative inverse.

00:07:36.348 --> 00:07:38.072
Alright.

00:07:38.072 --> 00:07:46.309
Next, for mental exercise, you can try to prove that -(-a) == a.

00:07:46.309 --> 00:07:54.278
Or that getting the additive inverse is very simple: you just need to multiply by -1.

00:07:54.278 --> 00:07:58.388
And -1 is the multiplicative inverse of 1.

00:08:00.197 --> 00:08:03.275
Similarly, you can prove this carefully.

00:08:03.275 --> 00:08:07.526
That when you take the additive inverse element of a product,

00:08:07.526 --> 00:08:13.954
you can take the additive inverse of one of these two factors.

00:08:13.954 --> 00:08:16.582
And many other familiar things can also be proven.

00:08:16.582 --> 00:08:20.758
For example, if you have an equality a*b1 == a*b2,

00:08:20.758 --> 00:08:25.780
you can cancel out "a" and get b1 = b2.

00:08:25.780 --> 00:08:30.299
Look how many steps you need to take here to prove it.

00:08:30.299 --> 00:08:34.986
With numbers, we're already used to these things,

00:08:34.986 --> 00:08:42.386
but here, when we're abstractly forming the concept of a number field, we need to prove it.

00:08:42.386 --> 00:08:46.570
Pause, look at the individual steps.

00:08:46.570 --> 00:08:48.473
What I want to emphasize here.

00:08:48.473 --> 00:08:54.868
Some simple things, which might be more intuitive and understandable.

00:08:54.868 --> 00:08:55.949
Look.

00:08:55.949 --> 00:09:00.770
We have a law that for every number "a", there's a multiplicative inverse "b".

00:09:00.770 --> 00:09:04.050
Let's take this multiplicative inverse and write this equation.

00:09:04.050 --> 00:09:07.189
Now let's multiply both sides by c.

00:09:07.189 --> 00:09:10.732
Next, we can move the parentheses here

00:09:10.732 --> 00:09:18.869
and get that for any element "c", there's a "d" such that "a * d == c".

00:09:18.869 --> 00:09:20.407
Alright...

00:09:20.407 --> 00:09:23.319
But how else can we interpret this?

00:09:23.319 --> 00:09:26.271
This can be interpreted as follows.

00:09:26.271 --> 00:09:35.312
In each row and column of the addition and multiplication tables, all numbers of our number field must appear.

00:09:35.312 --> 00:09:37.219
All the elements.

00:09:37.219 --> 00:09:44.853
So, let me try to formulate the property of a number field using the example of these addition and multiplication tables.

00:09:44.853 --> 00:09:51.367
First: these tables should be symmetric with respect to the diagonal.

00:09:51.367 --> 00:09:56.389
This is the commutativity law of addition and multiplication.

00:09:56.389 --> 00:10:07.038
Second: in each row and column of the addition and multiplication tables, all numbers must be present.

00:10:07.038 --> 00:10:13.926
This is essentially a consequence of the fact that there's an additive inverse for any number.

00:10:13.926 --> 00:10:21.817
And that for any "c" and any "a" not equal to zero, there exists a "d" such that a * d = c.

00:10:21.817 --> 00:10:23.789
This is what we just showed.

00:10:23.789 --> 00:10:27.601
The distributive law (this one here)

00:10:27.601 --> 00:10:37.044
is already challenging to demonstrate and explain using only these two tables.

00:10:37.044 --> 00:10:43.956
The fact that all numbers are present in each row of the multiplication table means,

00:10:43.956 --> 00:10:49.586
that there are no non-zero numbers "a" and "b" such that a * b = 0.

00:10:49.586 --> 00:10:53.401
Otherwise, there would be a zero somewhere in the multiplication table,

00:10:53.401 --> 00:11:03.043
and it would be impossible to place all possible values of the number field in this row.

00:11:03.043 --> 00:11:11.051
Okay. After going through all these pedantic discussions, we move on to interesting things.

00:11:11.051 --> 00:11:13.038
What interesting things?

00:11:13.038 --> 00:11:16.284
Interesting things are equations. You won't believe it! =)

00:11:16.284 --> 00:11:20.468
So, first: how to solve a linear equation?

00:11:20.468 --> 00:11:24.971
The answer is this: if you have a linear equation ax + b = 0,

00:11:24.971 --> 00:11:29.554
you need to take the additive inverse of "a", multiply it by "b",

00:11:29.554 --> 00:11:35.461
and then take the additive inverse of that number.

00:11:35.461 --> 00:11:39.766
Next, x^2 = a. Or taking the square root.

00:11:39.766 --> 00:11:44.185
Here we immediately encounter something very interesting.

00:11:44.185 --> 00:11:49.720
Let's live in these fields for now, fields which involve modular arithmetic.

00:11:49.720 --> 00:11:53.703
Modular arithmetic with respect to "p", where "p" is prime.

00:11:53.703 --> 00:12:00.538
Let's generate a thousand prime numbers and another ten thousand prime numbers,

00:12:00.538 --> 00:12:06.151
and see what squares modulo "p" can be.

00:12:06.151 --> 00:12:13.417
And we see that when we take all possible numbers from 0 to 12, square them,

00:12:13.417 --> 00:12:15.960
we don't get all possible numbers.

00:12:15.960 --> 00:12:17.331
And that's not surprising.

00:12:17.331 --> 00:12:22.955
If we take 2 and -2, the additive inverse of 2 exists,

00:12:22.955 --> 00:12:27.575
square them, we get 4.

00:12:27.575 --> 00:12:32.780
Thus, in these squares, each number is gotten  twice.

00:12:32.780 --> 00:12:39.175
And here, only half of the numbers from 1 to p - 1 are present.

00:12:39.175 --> 00:12:44.397
Here's 3. This means that x^2 = 3 has a solution.

00:12:44.397 --> 00:12:50.289
But there's no number 11. This means that x^2 = 11 has no solution.

00:12:50.289 --> 00:12:54.607
So you can't extract the square root of 11.

00:12:54.607 --> 00:12:56.581
Alright.

00:12:56.581 --> 00:13:03.848
Here we have the function DeleteDuplicates, and we see the unique elements, of which there are (p - 1)/2.

00:13:03.848 --> 00:13:11.174
Only half of the numbers from 1 to p are squares.

00:13:11.341 --> 00:13:15.123
And these numbers are called quadratic residues.

00:13:15.123 --> 00:13:19.777
And all others, except zero, are called quadratic non-residues.

00:13:19.777 --> 00:13:25.856
Let's calculate for each prime number p,

00:13:25.856 --> 00:13:34.357
whether -1, which is the same as p - 1, is a quadratic residue.

00:13:34.357 --> 00:13:45.035
So, 1 – when the prime number p, shown on the X-axis, has -1 as a quadratic residue,

00:13:45.035 --> 00:13:51.456
and -1 – if -1 is a quadratic non-residue.

00:13:51.456 --> 00:13:55.004
Some prime numbers yield 1, some yield -1.

00:13:55.004 --> 00:14:01.898
For example, modulo 5, -1 is a quadratic residue,

00:14:01.898 --> 00:14:06.719
while modulo 7, -1 is a quadratic non-residue.

00:14:06.719 --> 00:14:08.934
Modulo 11 – again a non-residue.

00:14:08.934 --> 00:14:11.812
Modulo 13 – a residue.

00:14:11.812 --> 00:14:19.272
Let's define the function HasSqrt as follows:

00:14:19.272 --> 00:14:25.693
q is a quadratic residue modulo p.

00:14:25.693 --> 00:14:32.683
This function, HasSqrt, is actually called the Legendre symbol,

00:14:32.683 --> 00:14:37.466
but in Mathematica, this function is called the JacobiSymbol.

00:14:37.466 --> 00:14:42.388
And it works not only for prime p. I'll explain that later.

00:14:42.388 --> 00:14:50.993
By the way, first, we can again use my favorite Accumulate...

00:14:50.993 --> 00:14:58.125
and see how prime numbers balance between those for which -1 is a quadratic residue

00:14:58.125 --> 00:15:02.745
and those for which it's not.

00:15:02.745 --> 00:15:04.618
Balancing in some way...

00:15:04.618 --> 00:15:10.176
Here we took a certain number of prime numbers up to 8000.

00:15:10.176 --> 00:15:16.169
And the balance here reached -15, but now it's back to -5.

00:15:16.169 --> 00:15:20.411
We can take more...

00:15:20.411 --> 00:15:22.820
Though calculating this will take quite a while.

00:15:22.820 --> 00:15:27.270
Here it's more appropriate to use, of course, the JacobiSymbol function.

00:15:27.270 --> 00:15:30.717
Let me do that.

00:15:30.717 --> 00:15:36.000
JacobiSymbol[-1, p]

00:15:36.000 --> 00:15:39.208
This is computed instantly.

00:15:39.208 --> 00:15:43.858
And here you can calculate it for very large numbers.

00:15:43.858 --> 00:15:51.542
I'll make a separate video about this balance between two kinds of prime numbers.

00:15:51.542 --> 00:15:54.134
Now, let's think about something else.

00:15:54.134 --> 00:15:55.840
What about cubes?

00:15:55.840 --> 00:16:02.894
I took cubes of all numbers from 1 to 12 and got only 4 numbers.

00:16:02.894 --> 00:16:06.000
So, all other numbers are not cubes.

00:16:06.000 --> 00:16:14.472
And if we write the equation x^3 == 11 modulo 13,

00:16:14.472 --> 00:16:17.197
we have an empty set of solutions.

00:16:17.197 --> 00:16:22.688
And if we write 8, how many solutions will there be?

00:16:26.138 --> 00:16:34.009
Here it gives solutions from real numbers or complex numbers.

00:16:34.009 --> 00:16:42.000
And in this way, it provides a solution from the number field F13.

00:16:42.000 --> 00:16:44.719
Alright... Let's take 17.

00:16:44.719 --> 00:16:47.363
Here we have all the numbers.

00:16:47.363 --> 00:16:54.621
Meaning, by cubing all numbers modulo 17, we get all the numbers again.

00:16:54.621 --> 00:16:56.729
And modulo 19?

00:16:56.729 --> 00:16:59.171
And modulo 23?

00:16:59.171 --> 00:17:05.432
Here we notice a pattern that the number of numbers,

00:17:05.432 --> 00:17:11.083
either all or (p - 1)/3,

00:17:11.083 --> 00:17:14.832
all of this depends on whether p - 1 is divisible by 3 or not.

00:17:14.832 --> 00:17:19.318
If p - 1 is divisible by 3, then you need to divide it by 3 and get the answer.

00:17:19.318 --> 00:17:22.096
That's how many cubes there will be.

00:17:22.096 --> 00:17:27.553
And if it's not divisible, then the cubes cover all numbers.

00:17:27.553 --> 00:17:29.527
Interesting observation.

00:17:29.527 --> 00:17:31.702
Moving on.

00:17:31.702 --> 00:17:36.000
Let's build a table of powers modulo p.

00:17:36.000 --> 00:17:39.587
Each row here represents a power.

00:17:39.587 --> 00:17:44.743
Here's 1^1, 1^2, 1^3, 1^4, and so on.

00:17:44.743 --> 00:17:49.798
And these are powers of two. 1, 2, 4, 8... At first, everything goes as expected.

00:17:49.798 --> 00:17:55.725
Then it should be 16, but 16 modulo 13 is 3. And so on.

00:17:55.725 --> 00:17:58.086
An immediate discovery:

00:17:58.086 --> 00:18:02.203
Row #12 contains all ones.

00:18:02.203 --> 00:18:04.110
Is this a coincidence or not?

00:18:04.110 --> 00:18:06.620
Let's take 19.

00:18:06.620 --> 00:18:09.498
No, it seems, this is not a coincidence.

00:18:09.498 --> 00:18:11.833
This is called Fermat's Little Theorem.

00:18:11.833 --> 00:18:19.903
Any number to the power of p - 1 modulo p equals 1. Except 0.

00:18:19.903 --> 00:18:24.456
Here I added a column with the number k for convenience.

00:18:24.456 --> 00:18:26.698
Meaning...

00:18:26.698 --> 00:18:30.547
in this row are the numbers "a",

00:18:30.547 --> 00:18:33.745
in this row are the numbers "k",

00:18:33.745 --> 00:18:38.151
and in the middle is the number "a^k".

00:18:38.151 --> 00:18:40.895
What do we see for 13?

00:18:40.895 --> 00:18:48.395
Here, in the third row (these are the cubes), we have only the numbers 1, 8, 12, and 5.

00:18:48.395 --> 00:18:50.970
And each number appears 3 times.

00:18:50.970 --> 00:18:58.772
And indeed, when we ask for Solve modulo 13, we get three answers.

00:18:58.772 --> 00:19:03.188
And modulo 19?

00:19:03.188 --> 00:19:08.336
And modulo 17 – one.

00:19:08.336 --> 00:19:11.074
Alright... Moving on.

00:19:11.074 --> 00:19:14.253
Here's the mentioned Fermat's Little Theorem.

00:19:14.253 --> 00:19:18.967
That the row p - 1 contains all ones.

00:19:18.967 --> 00:19:25.161
And the row numbered "p" will again contain 1, 2, 3, 4, 5, and so on up to 12.

00:19:25.161 --> 00:19:29.251
Meaning x^p will be equal to "x".

00:19:29.251 --> 00:19:35.378
And this equality is true for all numbers. Not just for 1, 2, 3, 4... 12, but also for 0.

00:19:35.378 --> 00:19:39.897
In a sense, it's a special equation,

00:19:39.897 --> 00:19:45.187
whose roots are all the elements of the number field.

00:19:45.187 --> 00:19:46.663
Alright.

00:19:46.663 --> 00:19:48.670
Next, we notice the following.

00:19:48.670 --> 00:19:54.596
Here we observe either 1 or -1. 12 is -1.

00:19:54.596 --> 00:20:01.978
And, furthermore, even clear for whom this expression is equal to 1.

00:20:01.978 --> 00:20:07.390
If the number "a" itself, which is here, is a square of some other number "b",

00:20:07.390 --> 00:20:12.134
for example, 4 is 2^2,

00:20:12.134 --> 00:20:16.285
then we will have (b^2)^6 drawn here,

00:20:16.285 --> 00:20:19.457
meaning b^12 is drawn here.

00:20:19.457 --> 00:20:24.873
And any number raised to the 12th power gives 1, so there's a 1 here.

00:20:24.873 --> 00:20:32.624
So, for numbers that are squares (quadratic residues), there will definitely be ones here.

00:20:32.624 --> 00:20:36.775
This is just our observation. An interesting observation.

00:20:36.775 --> 00:20:44.007
So, here... LegendreSymbol, which is denoted like this.

00:20:44.007 --> 00:20:51.574
It can actually be explicitly written as n^((p-1)/2).

00:20:51.574 --> 00:20:55.926
But we should use not 1 and 12, but 1 and -1.

00:20:55.926 --> 00:20:58.536
Alright.

00:20:58.536 --> 00:21:01.582
The next interesting observation.

00:21:01.582 --> 00:21:06.557
The Legendre Symbol is a multiplicative function.

00:21:06.557 --> 00:21:09.803
This immediately follows from this expression.

00:21:09.803 --> 00:21:11.844
Look.

00:21:11.844 --> 00:21:13.885
m^((p-1)/2)

00:21:13.885 --> 00:21:17.828
And this expression is n^((p-1)/2).

00:21:17.828 --> 00:21:23.680
So their product is (m*n)^((p-1)/2).

00:21:23.680 --> 00:21:27.596
Yes... just in case... I should explicitly mention this.

00:21:27.596 --> 00:21:30.642
We haven't proven this formula.

00:21:30.642 --> 00:21:34.927
We discovered it for some "p", but nothing more.

00:21:34.927 --> 00:21:37.571
We also haven't proven Fermat's Little Theorem.

00:21:37.571 --> 00:21:40.683
That these are all ones here.

00:21:40.683 --> 00:21:43.260
But there's nothing wrong with that.

00:21:43.260 --> 00:21:48.134
Let it be homework – to prove these two statements.

00:21:48.134 --> 00:21:50.242
But what I want to say...

00:21:50.242 --> 00:21:59.295
I sometimes start treating experimentally discovered facts for some number of "n" as truth.

00:21:59.295 --> 00:22:00.900
I postpone it for later.

00:22:00.900 --> 00:22:04.624
Maybe, at some point, I'll manage to prove or disprove these discovered facts,

00:22:04.624 --> 00:22:12.856
but there's nothing wrong with considering discovered facts as truth for a while.

00:22:12.856 --> 00:22:17.653
Legendre Symbol, for example. There was a very interesting story behind it.

00:22:17.653 --> 00:22:24.000
Pafnuty Chebyshev, whose polynomials I recently talked about, long ago discovered

00:22:24.000 --> 00:22:29.263
that if we consider the Legendre Symbol for -1,

00:22:29.263 --> 00:22:33.816
that is, consider prime numbers for which √ -1 is extracted,

00:22:33.816 --> 00:22:38.194
and prime numbers for which √ -1 is not extracted,

00:22:38.194 --> 00:22:43.316
then we'll find a certain imbalance between the two classes of prime numbers.

00:22:43.316 --> 00:22:49.613
Prime numbers for which √ -1 is not extracted are slightly more,

00:22:49.613 --> 00:22:52.122
if we take prime numbers &lt; n.

00:22:52.122 --> 00:22:56.340
For small n, and even for fairly large n.

00:22:56.340 --> 00:22:59.838
Let me explain.

00:22:59.838 --> 00:23:07.104
Let's consider the Legendre Symbol for -1.

00:23:08.645 --> 00:23:09.569
So...

00:23:09.569 --> 00:23:14.725
Let's call the team1 prime numbers &lt; n,

00:23:14.725 --> 00:23:16.725
for which this equation holds,

00:23:16.725 --> 00:23:19.335
and team2 prime numbers,

00:23:19.335 --> 00:23:21.979
for which this equation holds.

00:23:21.979 --> 00:23:27.027
Even then, in Chebyshev's time, this theorem was already known,

00:23:27.027 --> 00:23:38.710
called the "Prime Number Theorem of Arithmetic Progressions".

00:23:38.710 --> 00:23:47.580
But Chebyshev noticed that very often, for most n, team2 dominates.

00:23:47.580 --> 00:23:56.254
And then, in 1962, these two mathematicians, Knappowski and Turán, conjectured

00:23:56.254 --> 00:24:00.605
that this inequality is almost always true.

00:24:00.605 --> 00:24:09.344
This means that when you have X and you take any n &lt;= X,

00:24:09.344 --> 00:24:18.720
then the ratio of the fraction of n where team2 wins over team1,

00:24:18.720 --> 00:24:27.334
tends to 100% as X approaches infinity.

00:24:27.334 --> 00:24:37.047
A complex statement, as it involves using two large numbers, X and n,

00:24:37.047 --> 00:24:43.308
and considering the proportion of choices where n &lt; X.

00:24:43.308 --> 00:24:51.344
But then Littlewood came and relatively recently, in 1995 or 1994, proved,

00:24:51.344 --> 00:24:55.059
proved that no...

00:24:55.059 --> 00:25:00.516
This fraction of cases where team2 wins

00:25:00.516 --> 00:25:06.636
actually doesn't have a limit as it approaches infinity,

00:25:06.636 --> 00:25:10.287
but he was able to prove that there's a certain cunning measure,

00:25:10.287 --> 00:25:13.167
namely, 1/n,

00:25:13.167 --> 00:25:17.787
that, in this measure, the size of this team2 is still larger,

00:25:17.787 --> 00:25:20.866
and it's around 0.996... something like that.

00:25:20.866 --> 00:25:22.940
A very complex statement.

00:25:22.940 --> 00:25:28.805
Anyway, despite these two gentlemen making an incorrect assumption,

00:25:28.805 --> 00:25:31.683
their reputation remained unaffected.

00:25:31.683 --> 00:25:34.533
Their karma wasn't diminished at all.

00:25:34.533 --> 00:25:41.139
On the contrary, it's interesting to make assumptions and then either prove or disprove them.

00:25:41.139 --> 00:25:49.510
And a bit more about x^m.

00:25:49.510 --> 00:25:52.656
Let p - 1 be divisible by m,

00:25:52.656 --> 00:25:58.950
then we can show that x^m cannot cover all numbers.

00:25:58.950 --> 00:26:07.548
And this would mean that "x^m = a" will have no solutions for some values of "a".

00:26:07.548 --> 00:26:10.879
See how this is proved.

00:26:10.879 --> 00:26:12.504
It's proven by contradiction.

00:26:12.504 --> 00:26:17.866
Let x^m cover all numbers from 1 to p - 1.

00:26:17.866 --> 00:26:26.551
Then we can raise "x^m" to the power of "n" and get that

00:26:26.551 --> 00:26:30.000
the entire expression should be equal to 1.

00:26:30.000 --> 00:26:35.833
If x^m covers all numbers from 1 to p - 1,

00:26:35.833 --> 00:26:45.858
then the equation y^n = 1 must have p - 1 roots.

00:26:45.858 --> 00:26:50.175
Indeed, we substitute "y" with "x^m",

00:26:50.175 --> 00:26:52.431
"x" ranges from 1 to p - 1,

00:26:52.431 --> 00:26:59.362
and we get here, as we assumed, the same numbers: 1, 2, 3, ..., p - 1.

00:26:59.362 --> 00:27:05.656
They are just rearranged amongst each other when raised to the power of m.

00:27:05.656 --> 00:27:13.523
But the polynomial y^n - 1 cannot have more than "n" distinct roots.

00:27:13.523 --> 00:27:17.177
This is true for real numbers, for complex numbers.

00:27:17.177 --> 00:27:20.069
This is true for any field of numbers.

00:27:20.069 --> 00:27:22.545
Including finite fields.

00:27:22.545 --> 00:27:26.327
Think about why. Try to convince yourself of that.

00:27:26.327 --> 00:27:30.465
And there's another true statement in the opposite direction.

00:27:30.465 --> 00:27:33.477
Let p - 1 not be divisible by "m".

00:27:33.477 --> 00:27:39.697
Moreover, let p - 1 be coprime with the number "m".

00:27:39.697 --> 00:27:45.087
From this, it immediately follows that x^m covers all numbers from 1 to p - 1.

00:27:45.087 --> 00:27:47.850
How is this proved?

00:27:47.850 --> 00:27:49.712
Look.

00:27:49.712 --> 00:27:54.576
If "m" and p - 1 are coprime,

00:27:54.576 --> 00:27:57.413
then we can find such an "n"

00:27:57.413 --> 00:28:03.205
that n * n has a remainder of 1 when divided by p - 1.

00:28:03.205 --> 00:28:06.853
Next, we consider this power.

00:28:06.853 --> 00:28:10.763
Then we recall that m * n has a remainder of 1.

00:28:10.763 --> 00:28:12.184
What does that mean?

00:28:12.184 --> 00:28:18.177
This means that m * n can be represented in this form.

00:28:18.177 --> 00:28:21.667
But x^(p-1),

00:28:21.667 --> 00:28:25.744
if we take only this part of the power,

00:28:25.744 --> 00:28:27.751
gives us 1,

00:28:27.751 --> 00:28:31.533
so we can drop this part from the power

00:28:31.533 --> 00:28:33.641
and we get x^1.

00:28:33.641 --> 00:28:42.313
So x^(m*n) = x^1 modulo p.

00:28:42.313 --> 00:28:49.009
x^m cannot cover not all numbers.

00:28:49.009 --> 00:28:52.925
If not all numbers are encountered in this bracket,

00:28:52.925 --> 00:28:56.707
then we sort of lose information about x,

00:28:56.707 --> 00:29:00.716
and we can't recover it when raised to the power of n.

00:29:00.716 --> 00:29:12.905
Here, it turns out that (x^m)^n is exactly equal to x, thus retaining all information about x.

00:29:12.905 --> 00:29:15.448
So, the promised homework.

00:29:15.448 --> 00:29:21.544
Let's better write the homework in Russian.

00:29:21.544 --> 00:29:25.868
Find a proof for Fermat's Little Theorem.

00:29:25.868 --> 00:29:31.961
That x^(p-1) is congruent to 1 modulo p.

00:29:31.961 --> 00:29:34.795
One of the proofs is based on the following:

00:29:34.795 --> 00:29:38.175
take the a-th row of the multiplication table

00:29:38.175 --> 00:29:41.421
and multiply all the numbers present there.

00:29:41.421 --> 00:29:46.750
On one hand, this product is all the numbers from 1 to p - 1,

00:29:46.750 --> 00:29:52.980
on the other hand, it's equal to this product.

00:29:52.980 --> 00:29:59.293
And then you can work with this expression to prove Fermat's Little Theorem.

00:29:59.293 --> 00:30:05.221
And the next thing about the Legendre symbol.

00:30:05.221 --> 00:30:08.734
Prove that from these three statements,

00:30:08.734 --> 00:30:13.655
this decomposition of the polynomial x^(p-1) - 1,

00:30:13.655 --> 00:30:15.595
from Fermat's Little Theorem

00:30:15.595 --> 00:30:22.351
and the fact that a polynomial with k distinct roots can have degree k or greater,

00:30:22.351 --> 00:30:28.404
the formula for the Legendre symbol follows (from these three statements).

00:30:28.404 --> 00:30:32.797
That's all for today! Good luck to everyone! Goodbye!

